<p>To run the fetch request depending on a prop or state value, simply indi=
cate the required dependency in the dependencies argument: <code>useEffect(=
fetchSideEffect, [prop, stateValue])</code>.</p>
<h2 id=3D"6-conclusion" style=3D"position:relative"><a href=3D"https://dmit=
ripavlutin.com/react-useeffect-explanation/#6-conclusion" aria-label=3D"6 c=
onclusion permalink" class=3D"anchor before"><span></span></a>6. Conclusion=
</h2>
<p><code>useEffect(callback, dependencies)</code> is the hook that manages =
the side-effects in functional components. <code>callback</code> argument i=
s a function where to put the side-effect logic. <code>dependencies</code> =
is a list of dependencies of your side-effect: being props or state values.=
</p>
<p><code>useEffect(callback, dependencies)</code> invokes the <code>callbac=
k</code> after initial rendering (mounting), and on later renderings, if an=
y value inside <code>dependencies</code> has changed.</p>
<p>The next step to mastering <code>useEffect()</code> is to understand and=
 avoid <a href=3D"https://dmitripavlutin.com/react-useeffect-infinite-loop/=
">the infinite loop pitfall</a>.</p>
