  <span class=3D"pl-k">const</span> <span class=3D"pl-en">fetchMethod</span=
> <span class=3D"pl-c1">=3D</span> <span class=3D"pl-k">async</span> <span =
class=3D"pl-kos">(</span><span class=3D"pl-kos">)</span> <span class=3D"pl-=
c1">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"pl-kos">{</span>
    <span class=3D"pl-c">// fetching logic</span>
  <span class=3D"pl-kos">}</span><span class=3D"pl-kos">;</span>
  <span class=3D"pl-en">fetchMethod</span><span class=3D"pl-kos">(</span><s=
pan class=3D"pl-kos">)</span><span class=3D"pl-kos">;</span>
<span class=3D"pl-kos">}</span><span class=3D"pl-kos">,</span> <span class=
=3D"pl-kos">[</span><span class=3D"pl-s1">id</span><span class=3D"pl-kos">]=
</span><span class=3D"pl-kos">)</span></pre></div>
<ol start=3D"2" dir=3D"auto">
<li>If you see the same fetching logic being shared between multiple compon=
ents, you can extract the fetching into a <a href=3D"https://reactjs.org/do=
cs/hooks-custom.html" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" target=3D"_top">custom ho=
ok</a>. Extracting the side-effect logic out of the component makes the com=
ponent simpler, as well conforms to the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) princip=
le.</li>
</ol>
